/**
 * What milk becomes: every product as a node and every making-of as an arrow,
 * milk itself large in the middle and what is made from it pulled out round
 * it in rings, one ring a step of making further out. Redrawn from
 * Wikimedia Commons' "Milch.svg" (Milchprodukte und deren Herstellung), in
 * English, without its fat percentages or its coloured regions, and filled
 * out from a diagram of my own: homogenising, the strained yoghurts, the
 * fermented milks of the steppe, and whey protein.
 *
 *   <DeckViz><MilkGraph /></DeckViz>
 *   <DeckViz><MilkGraph focus="rawCream" /></DeckViz>
 *
 * With `focus`, a product's id, it opens on that product and everything
 * made from it, framed and lit, rather than on the whole tree; a card can
 * move it with `vizProps={{ focus: … }}`, or back to the whole with null.
 *
 * Laid out in the browser as a tree on a grid, worked out once so that no
 * two nodes overlap, each arrow saying how the one is made from the other. The products are a plain list below,
 * each saying what it is made from; a product left out by deleting its line
 * is reported at build time by anything still made from it.
 */

/**
 * A product: the name drawn on its node, a line break where it wraps, and
 * every other name it goes by, here or elsewhere in the world. The other
 * names are kept with it rather than drawn, so a product found under another
 * name is folded in here instead of becoming a node of its own. Many came
 * out of a round of research and are not all checked yet (see
 * reports/Milk products missing from graph.md).
 *
 * `from` is what it is made from, one parent and no more: of the ways it
 * can be made, the one that matters most, from the more everyday product
 * and from its own family where there is a choice. The other ways are kept
 * in `alsoFrom` as the other names are in `aka`, not drawn. Milk and the
 * other milks beside it are made from nothing. `step` is how it is made from
 * `from`, in a few words, written on the arrow. What one step makes
 * besides is made with it, as a split (see SPLITS): setting milk with rennet
 * makes the curd, and the whey drains off.
 *
 * A product with a `scale` is a kind measured along it rather than a
 * product of its own: drawn with a ruler under it, and what is made from it
 * hung below, each straight down from where its own `at` is on the ruler;
 * cultured cream, and its creams by how much fat they have.
 *
 * A product with `panel` is a family of cheeses too many for a list: its
 * examples are drawn in a panel under it, each placed by how long it is
 * ripened across and how firm it is down (see EXAMPLE_AXES); what is made
 * from it branches off to its right as from any other.
 */
type Product = {
	label: string;
	aka?: string[];
	examples?: string[];
	from?: string;
	alsoFrom?: string[];
	step?: string;
	scale?: { name: string; unit: string; to: number; every: number };
	at?: number;
	panel?: boolean;
	// Drawn as the header of a map of the world's cheeses, each where it is made (see WORLD).
	map?: boolean;
	// Drawn just below the branch of this sibling, as its kin, whatever their sizes.
	below?: string;
	// A remark worth having at a glance, behind an info mark on its pill's bottom right corner.
	note?: string;
	// Not a product of its own but another drawn again where it is also made, with a link
	// mark on it: pressed, the graph goes to that one. Its names and its card are that one's.
	link?: string;
};

