	'Hershey bar': "Introduced 1900; the Hershey process is a trade secret, speculated to involve partly lipolysed milk giving a tangy butyric note",
	'Pinkberry': "Opened January 2005 in West Hollywood by Shelly Hwang and Young Lee; an LA Times lab test found far fewer live cultures than the 10 million per gram the National Yogurt Association requires, and it got the Live and Active Cultures seal on 17 April 2008 after changing the recipe",
	'Baileys Irish Cream': "Cream, Irish whiskey and cocoa; makers state a 24-month shelf life with no preservatives",
	'Amarula': "Cream with the spirit of fermented South African marula fruit",
	'Chocolate milk': "Cocoa settles out of milk, so it is held up with a stabiliser, usually kappa-carrageenan (forms a network with casein at the milk’s own pH) or microcrystalline cellulose",
	'Masala milk': "Bottled spiced milk, popular in the Indian subcontinent",
	'Hong Kong-style milk tea': "Strained black tea (Ceylon) finished with evaporated milk; developed under British colonial influence",
	'Suutei tsai': "Mongolian salty milk tea; in the grassland form crushed tea is steeped, boiled again, milk stirred in and salt added",
	'Masala chai': "Spiced milk tea of the Indian subcontinent",
	'Horlicks': "Began as Horlick’s Infant and Invalids Food (1873); UK recipe lists malted wheat 46 %, malted barley 26 %, wheat flour, dried whey, dried skimmed milk. Largest market India, where it is a breakfast drink; Unilever owns it outside the UK",
	'Ovaltine': "Developed 1904 in Bern by Albert Wander; name from Latin ovum (egg) and malt; malt extract, sugar and whey, some flavours with cocoa",
	'Milo': "Nestlé, 1934, Australia (Thomas Mayne): malt extract, full-cream milk powder, cocoa, sugar; recipe varies by country",
	'Viking Melk': "Norwegian tinned evaporated milk, invented by Olav Johan Sopp in 1891; a Nestlé brand since 1897",
	'Carnation': "Nestlé brand (licensed to Smucker’s in Canada); the Carnation plant opened in Kent, Washington in 1899",
	'PET': "Smucker’s brand",
	'Butter lamp': 'Burns clarified butter, in Tibetan Buddhism clarified yak butter, as fuel; now often vegetable oil.',
	'Tibetan torma': 'Hand-moulded from butter mixed with flour and dye, not built cold on a frame.',
	"Elmer's Glue": "Borden's Elmer's glues were casein-based at first; Borden moved to synthetics such as PVA towards the end of the 20th century",
	'Aircraft glue': 'Casein glue was used in aircraft woodwork as late as the de Havilland Albatross airliner of 1939',
	'Blancmange': 'Milk or cream and sugar thickened with rice flour, gelatin or cornflour, often almond flavoured, set in a mould; medieval ones had shredded capon; traces back to the older Middle Eastern muhallebi',
	'Tavuk göğsü': 'Turkish, served to Ottoman sultans in Topkapı Palace; the Apicius origin claim is not supported by surviving copies (Topçu 2023); Arab origin more likely',
	'Kazandibi': 'Caramelised milk pudding: traditionally made by burning the bottom of tavuk göğsü; a variant uses muhallebi; name = bottom of the cauldron',
	'Vla': 'Dutch dairy dessert from fresh milk; traditionally eggs, sugar and milk, industrial vla often cornstarch instead of eggs; first documented in the 13th century',
	'Semolina pudding': 'Semolina cooked with milk, or milk and water; eaten in Europe since Roman times (Apicius describes a farina porridge); instant versions use milk powder',
	'Sütlaç': 'Turkish rice pudding, often baked (fırın sütlaç) until the top browns; name from sütlü aş, dish with milk; may be sweetened with sugar or pekmez; eaten by Sephardic Jews at Shavuot',
	'Arroz con leche': 'The Spanish and Latin American rice pudding, usually flavoured with cinnamon',
	'Arroz doce': 'Portuguese and Brazilian sweet rice pudding',
	'Mathura peda': 'The peda originated in Mathura, Uttar Pradesh; thick semi-soft brown balls of khoa, sugar and cardamom; the word comes from Sanskrit piṇḍa, a lump; Bhojanakutūhala (17th c.) describes milk and sugar with cardamom, cloves and pepper',
	'Dharwad peda': 'Karnataka; GI tag number 80; about 175 years old; milk heated and stirred continuously with sugar; started by the Thakur family who came from Unnao',
	'Khurchan': 'From khurchana, Hindi for to scrape; Old Delhi and Khurja, Mathura; Not reduced to a solid like rabri: the scraped skins are the product',
	'Varenaya sgushchyonka': 'In the USSR the tin was boiled unopened at home, with a risk of bursting; industrial production began only in the 1990s',
	'Chongos zamoranos': 'Milk is set with rennet and served on a sweet brown milky syrup; origin attributed to colonial convents of Zamora, Michoacán',
	'Ostkaka': 'Hälsinge ostkaka is pressed and smooth, almost halloumi-like; Småland ostkaka is soft-grained, with cream, eggs and almonds. Not American cheesecake',
	'Pastry cream': 'Custard thickened with cornstarch or flour as well, so it can be boiled; thick enough to fill éclairs',
	'Crème caramel': 'Egg custard baked in a bain-marie over a layer of caramel, then turned out so the caramel runs down; called flan in Spanish-speaking countries. First documented in its modern form in Reboul, La cuisinière provençale, 1897.',
	'Crème brûlée': 'Rich custard under a hard layer of caramelised sugar; earliest known recipe Massialot 1691, egg yolks and milk with a pinch of flour; some versions use cream instead of milk',
	'Crema catalana': 'Cooked on the hob (not baked) with cinnamon and lemon zest, usually thickened with cornflour, chilled, then caramelised on top; made from milk, egg yolks, sugar; cannot be frozen',
	'Leche flan': 'Philippine caramel custard of condensed milk and egg yolks, usually steamed, rarely baked',
	'Natillas': 'In Spain a pouring custard of milk, sugar, vanilla, egg yolks and cinnamon, often with a María biscuit on top; in Colombia natilla has no eggs',
	'Fudge': 'Softer than caramel and firmer than fondant. Modern fudge comes from 19th century USA; Cornish fudge uses clotted cream instead of milk, and the Scottish tablet is grainier and brittle.',
	'Booza': 'Syrian, documented in Damascus since at least the late 19th century (Bakdash by 1895)',
	'Bastani': 'Iran; a yolk-rich custard base like French-style ice cream',
	'Semifreddo': 'Italian, "half-cold"; earliest mention Antonio Nebbia, Il cuoco maceratese, 1779, semifreddo fior di latte of milk, sugar and cream',
	'Hangop': 'Traditionally from buttermilk, today mostly yoghurt; not Jan-in-de-zak',
	'Clabber': 'Came to the American South with the Ulster Scots and was once used as a quick leavener before baking powder; making it virtually stopped once milk was pasteurised',
	'Formaggio di fossa': 'Rarely buffalo milk too; matured 20-30 days before burying per Wikipedia (the PDO rules are reported as at least 80 days, sources differ)',
	'Montebore': 'Shaped as stacked rounds of decreasing diameter, after the ruined tower of Montebore castle; served at the 1489 wedding of Isabella of Aragon',
	'Crowdie': 'Traditional skim-milk cheese of the Highlands and Islands; Gruth dhu (black crowdie) is crowdie mixed with double cream and rolled in oatmeal and black pepper; Hramsa has wild garlic',
	'Schabziger': 'Made only in Glarus; the green comes from blue fenugreek (Schabzigerklee, Trigonella caerulea). Grated, never sliced',
	'Ricotta forte': 'Fermented by bacteria and yeast, like Greek kopanisti',
	'Gammelost': 'Ripens from the surface inward with a mould (Mucor mucedo) instead of a smear; about 1 % fat and 50 % protein',
	'Pultost': 'Spreadable (Loiten) and grainy (Hedemark, Lillehammer) types; stronger when aged',
	'Jānu siers': 'Eaten at Jāņi, the midsummer festival; under 30 % fat; a firm cylinder, not a spreadable cooked cheese',
	'Brousse du Rove': 'Made from whole milk, not from whey like other brousses; farm production only',
	'Caboc': 'Rennet-free; formed into a log and rolled in toasted pinhead oatmeal. The 15th-century Mariota de Ile story is family legend, not documented history',
	'Paskha': 'Moulded in a wooden pasochnitsa; eaten at the end of Lent',
	'Sernik': 'Polish cheesecake from twaróg (curd), eggs and butter, no cream or sour cream; dates to the 17th century; variants krakowski with lattice crust, królewski with cocoa crust, wiedeński crustless',
	'Japanese soufflé cheesecake': 'Cream cheese, butter, sugar and eggs; wobbly, airy like chiffon cake',
	'New York cheesecake': 'Made with cream cheese; of Ashkenazi Jewish origin in the 1930s',
	'Basque burnt cheesecake': 'Crustless, burnt custard; Santiago Rivera of La Viña, 1990',
	'No-bake cheesecake': 'Uncooked cream cheese, cream or gelatin on a biscuit base',
	'Rivella': 'Rivella Red, with 35 % whey',
	'Partly demineralised whey': 'About 25-30 % of the ash removed',
	'Demin 90 (D90)': 'About 90 % of the ash removed; supplier specifications differ, so no single ash figure',
	'Three-quarter fat butter': 'EU legal category (Reg. 1308/2013 Annex VII Part VII, Appendix II): 60-62 % milk fat; may be sold as reduced-fat or light butter',
	'Half-fat butter': 'EU legal category: 39-41 % milk fat; butter itself must be at least 80 % and under 90 %, water at most 16 %, non-fat solids at most 2 %; anything between is a dairy spread',
	'Dairy spread': 'EU legal category for milk-fat spreads outside the butter grades: under 39 %, 41-60 % or 62-80 % milk fat',
	'Rucava white butter': 'Latvian PGI (2018): made by hand with a special churning method; soft, fluffy, slightly acidic; eaten fresh with boiled potatoes or rye bread',
	"Beurre d'Isigny": 'AOP (PDO since 1996) from the Bay of Veys, Normandy; can be salted, and can be textured to suit puff pastry; creamy and hazelnut aromas',
	"Beurre d'Échiré": 'AOP Charentes-Poitou; made by the Échiré dairy cooperative since 1894 from milk of farms within about 50 km; AOP rules reportedly demand 18 h cream maturation and pH 4.7 (standard rules 12 h, pH 6) – the figures come from a French press article seen only in search summaries',
	'Beurre Rose': 'Luxembourg cultured milk butter, PDO since 2000; the name comes from the pink state quality label, not the butter; continuous churning, tested 15 times a year',
	'Vologda butter': "Russia's first protected designation of origin (2010); invented by Nikolai Vereshchagin, shown as 'Parisian butter' at Paris 1878 (gold medal); sweet, creamy, nutty",
	'Beurre noir': 'Black butter: a similar sauce cooked longer',
	'Crème Chantilly': 'Whipped cream sweetened with sugar and usually flavoured with vanilla',
	'Spray whipped cream': 'Aerosol can: nitrous oxide dissolves in the butterfat under pressure and foams as the can is released; CO2 would taste sour',
	'Devon clotted cream': 'Disputed origin with Cornwall; paler because the grass has less carotene (Wikipedia); in Devon the cream goes on the scone first, in Cornwall the jam; only Cornish clotted cream is a PDO (1998, min 55 % butterfat, milk from Cornwall)',
	'Brânză de burduf': 'Made from caş dospit (matured sweet curd), cut, salted and kneaded by hand; fir bark gives a resin taste. Softer fattier versions: brânză de putină, Cremă Focșani.',
	'Ezine': 'Mixed milk, about 40 % goat, 50 % sheep, 10 % cow; no starter; ripened in metal tins like Edirne cheese. First Turkish cheese registered in the EU.',
	'Majorero': 'Cured versions may include up to 15 % Canarian sheep milk; rind left natural or rubbed with oil, paprika or toasted gofio; edges show the imprint of the palm-leaf braid mould.',
	'Fiore Sardo': 'Raw milk, no starter added; named for the old wooden moulds carved with a flower. Raw-milk, uncooked curd (curd broken to millet-grain size, not cooked); firm from long ripening, grated after 6 months.',
	'Pecorino Sardo': 'Two types: dolce (20–60 d) and maturo (over 2 months). The only Sardinian PDO set with calf rennet.',
	'Roncal': 'Raw milk, made December to July; first Spanish cheese with a denominación de origen (1981). Pressed, uncooked: the curd stays at 30–37 °C; firm from 4+ months of ripening.',
	'Ossau-Iraty': 'Farm versions raw milk only. Pressed, uncooked: heating stays below 44 °C.',
	'Ladotyri Mytilinis': 'Ladotyri means oil cheese: after ripening it is submerged in olive oil, a preservation method common before household refrigerators. Mass-market version is called graviera.',
	'Parenica': 'Slovak PGI; wound into two spiral rolls joined in an S, usually smoked.',
	'Žázrivský korbáčik': 'Protected local variety (PGI) of korbáčik from the Žázrivá area in Orava; the generic korbáčik is made in many places.',
	'Korbáčik': 'Smoked and unsmoked, also salty or garlic versions.',
	'Oštiepok': 'The PGI allows it steamed or not, smoked or not; Goral oscypek is a different cheese (different ratios and process). Egg-, cone- or ellipse-shaped with ornaments pressed in.',
	'Pule': 'Needs goat milk: donkey milk has too little casein, especially kappa-casein, for rennet to set it alone. About 25 L of milk per kg; only a few dozen jennies are milked, by hand.',
	'Katiki Domokou': 'PDO; spreadable, low fat.',
	'Picodon': "Washed variant ('affiné lavé' / méthode Dieulefit) is dried, washed at least twice and kept in covered jars; sold from 30 days after renneting instead of 12.",
	'Graviera Kritis': 'Cretan PDO, goat milk at most about 20 %. Naxos graviera (PDO) is mostly cow milk, Agrafa the third PDO graviera. The name comes from Gruyère.',
	'Messmör': 'Swedish; whey only, boiled down until it caramelises, no cream or sugar.',
	'Getmessmör': 'Swedish; the same, from goat whey.',
	'Sýra': 'Soured whey kept in barrels for preserving food, and drunk after dilution.',
	'Tarag': 'Mongolian; fermented spontaneously at ambient temperature with a mixed lactobacillus, lactococcus and yeast flora, no S. thermophilus',
	'Galotyri': 'Greek PDO of Epirus and Thessaly; counted as neither cheese nor yoghurt; the industrial version uses a yoghurt starter, artisan ones sour naturally and are ripened.',
	'Sorke': 'Aged shanklish worked into a pungent paste, eaten with tomato and onion',
	'Chakka': 'Strained dahi, so mesophilic lactococcal, not thermophilic yoghurt; Shrikhand is made from it',
	'Suzma': 'In Central Asia strained qatiq (boiled milk set with its own starter), not Greek-style thermophilic yoghurt; Turkish süzme yoğurt is strained ordinary yoghurt',
	'Jocoque seco': 'The dried form of jocoque: strained curd dried, or kept in oil as balls',
	'Jocoque': 'The Mexican kind is a soured milk, traditionally spontaneous, not strained thermophilic yoghurt',
	'Afyon kaymagi': 'Raw milk; Turkish GI: at least 60 % fat and 65 % dry matter',
	'Muhallebi': 'Rice-flour or cornstarch-thickened milk pudding, scented with rose or orange-blossom water',
	'Double-skin milk': 'The skin that forms on cooled boiled milk is lifted aside, the milk is beaten with egg white and sugar and poured back under it, and steaming grows a second skin; a dessert, not a cream',
	'Queijo de coalho': 'Not heated hard like halloumi: the curd is cooked to 45–55 °C at most, and it holds its shape on a grill because it is firm and moist',
	'Queijo de manteiga': 'Made from acid-set milk curd, not from finished cheese, then melted with manteiga de garrafa',
	'Kesong puti': 'The vinegar version is not fermented and is slightly sour, the rennet version is firmer. Salted in the milk or in brine afterwards, sold wrapped in banana leaf',
	'Langtang yak cheese': 'Swiss-style cheese made at Kyangjin since the 1950s; taught by Swiss cheesemakers (Werner Schulthess, Sepp Dubach), Swiss project handed to the government in 1964',
	'Chechil': 'Made from skimmed, soured milk, scalded and pulled into strands, braided and kept in brine; Armenia and Georgia',
	'Tenili': 'The strands are packed tightly into a clay pot (tenili = stuffed) and matured about two months',
	'Imeruli': 'Cow milk, up to 20 % buffalo or goat; Georgian GI; young cheese, sold a day after brining',
	'Bouhezza': 'Made by filling a skin bag with salted lben and milk over weeks, not by packing pressed curd.',
	'Rumi': 'Raw milk, no starter; the name means Roman, but it is Egyptian.',
	'Gibna bayda': 'Salt is added to the milk before the rennet, as in Domiati.',
	'Mudafara': 'Stretched in boiled water but kept in brine like a feta.',
	'Caravane': 'Camel\'s milk sets poorly with rennet; the FAO sent a scientist to teach the dairy a method (Wikipedia, 1992).',
	'Guda': 'From Tusheti; the name means cheese from a bag; the skin is a young sheep hide with the wool shaved off',
	'Lighvan': 'Raw milk, no starter; Lighvan village, East Azerbaijan',
	'Jibneh Arabieh': 'Of the Bedouin first; mild, less salty than feta; also the filling of knafeh',
	'Otlu peynir': 'Raw milk; wild herbs mixed into the curd; brined and matured underground',
	'Jben': 'Moroccan and Algerian sources describe it as rennet-set or lactic-set depending on the maker; it sits here with rennet fresh cheeses.',
	'Klila': 'Dried, unlike soft chhurpi or ayib; the closest relative is hard chhurpi',
	'Karish': 'Drained cold, unlike Areesh, which is heated gently',
	'Dali ni horbo': 'Boiled and set with pineapple juice or papaya leaf juice (it also tames the strong smell, per Indonesia Travel); a Batak staple sold in the markets (onan) of Tapanuli',
	'Litsusu': 'Set with sap enzymes of the litsusu tree',
	'Cologanti': 'Set with sap enzymes of the litsusu tree',
	'Matsoni': 'Of Armenian origin (matz = sour, in Grigor Magistros, 11th century); on Armenia\'s intangible heritage list; Georgian matsoni a protected GI since 24 January 2012; the Caspian Sea yoghurt sold in Japan is said to be the same type, but a microbiota and viscosity comparison found them entirely different',
	'Shrikhand': 'Chakka worked with sugar, cardamom and saffron; sikarni in Nepal',
	'Sweet lassi': 'Made from set dahi, with its mesophilic starter, not from thermophilic yoghurt; sugar is stirred in instead of salt',
	'Meiji Bulgaria': 'Japan’s best-known plain yoghurt; named LB81 after its two strains, L. bulgaricus 2038 and S. thermophilus 1131 (last digits 8 and 1)',
	'Beijing yoghurt': 'Sweetened with sugar or honey and made with glutinous-rice wine; sold in small porcelain or ceramic jars with a deposit, today often in plastic cups',
	'Juju dhau': 'Newar sweet curd from Bhaktapur, Nepal: milk and brown sugar, set in clay pots',
	'Sho': 'Tibetan yoghurt, also churned for butter',
	'Aqit': 'Bedouin: milk churned in a goatskin, the buttermilk heated until it curdles, curd shaped into discs and dried in the shade at least a day; some accounts make it from boiled yoghurt instead (sources differ)',
	'Qatiq': 'Boiled milk fermented 6-10 h in a warm place; denser than ayran; strained gives suzma, dried suzma is kurut; used in the Uzbek chalop soup',
	'Yak butter': 'Churned from soured milk, not from separated cream; keeps up to a year away from air. Fat of butter tea and butter lamps',
	'Shar tos': 'Mongolian clarified butter, melted gently from tsagaan tos (white butter, made by straining and churning cream)',
	'Manteiga de garrafa': 'Cooked from cream, not from butter',
	'Makhan': 'Churned from dahi (set whole milk or malai), not from separated cream',
	'Zebda': 'Churned from raw milk that sours on its own, not from separated cream',
	'Kibe': 'Churned from soured whole milk, not from cream',
	'Pastillas de leche': 'Sugar and calamansi juice go in; origin San Miguel, Bulacan',
	'Phirni': 'Ground rice cooked in milk and eaten chilled',
	'Payasam': 'South Indian; often jaggery, coconut milk or vermicelli',
	'Payesh': 'Bengali; scented gobindobhog rice, often date-palm jaggery',
	'Basundi': 'Milk simmered to about half and sweetened; thinner than rabri, which is boiled down further with the skins folded in',
	'Palkova': 'Srivilliputhur palkova (GI): cow milk and sugar boiled down, traditionally over tamarind-wood fires',
	'So': 'Ancient Japanese dairy product of the 7th–10th centuries, made by boiling milk down to a solid; the picture shows a modern reconstruction',
	'Cuajada': 'Spanish counterpart of junket; the Brazilian coalhada is a different thing, a soured milk curdled with whey from earlier batches',
	'Medium-heat skim milk powder': 'The vast majority of skim milk powder is of this class (Technews review)',
	'Instant skim milk powder': 'Agglomerated so it dissolves in cold water',
	'Double-toned milk': 'Fat cut to 1.5 % (min.), SNF 9 %; pasteurised',
	'QMilch': 'Modern German fibre (Anke Domaske) made from dairy-waste milk; the maker says it uses no harsh chemicals, unlike the Lanital-type fibres hardened in formaldehyde (company statement)',
	'Erinoid': 'British trade name for casein plastic',
	'Lactoid': 'Trade name for casein plastic',
	'Aladdinite': 'American trade name for casein plastic',
	'Dondurma': "Maraş dondurma: milk, salep and sugar, no cream; pounded and stretched, not churned",
	'Frozen custard': 'Ice-cream mix with egg yolks (US standard: at least 1.4 %)',
	'Soft serve': 'The same kind of mix, drawn off at about -5 °C with more air',
	'Žinčica': "The Slovak name for the same whey drink, made from the whey left over from bryndza.",
	'Salted lassi': 'Made from set dahi, with its mesophilic starter, not from thermophilic yoghurt; the curd is broken up and salt and cumin go in after fermentation',
	'Renost': 'Reindeer milk is very rich (about 22 % fat, 10 % protein) but a doe gives about 1.5 cups a day, so production is tiny; it was often taken in coffee instead of cream.',
	'Leipäjuusto': 'Traditionally made from reindeer milk; today usually cow milk, preferably the first milk (colostrum) after calving, or goat',
	'String cheese': 'Low-moisture part-skim mozzarella, pulled into strings (US)',
	'Requeijão cremoso': 'Made from milk curd, not from finished cheese or whey',
	'Milch ab Hof': 'Ordinary raw milk sold only at the farm gate, not Vorzugsmilch; it must carry a boil-before-drinking notice',
	'Kasseri': 'Kashkaval (Balkans) and kaşar (Turkey) are the same kind of cheese',
	'Cotija': 'Salty, crumbly Mexican cheese; the milk is salted before the cheese is made, so it is not brine-ripened like feta',
	'Chaource': 'Set mainly by lactic acid (AOC: lactic coagulation of at least 12 h, slow spontaneous draining), then ripened under a white Penicillium candidum rind like a Brie',
	'Neufchâtel': 'The Norman cheese with a white mould rind; the American cream cheese called Neufchâtel is a different, unripened cheese (see Cream cheese)',
	'Vivinal GOS': 'FrieslandCampina; 57 % of infant formulas launched in 2025 contained GOS (NutraIngredients 2025)',
	MPC70: 'Grades are named by protein as a share of dry matter (MPC60 is 60 %); diafiltration, adding water to wash lactose out, is what takes MPC past about 70 %',
	MPC80: 'Up to 40 %, 60–70 % and 80 % or more protein are called low, medium and high protein powders; the US has no standard of identity for MPC (US Dairy Export Council technical report, seen in a search summary)',
	'Valio lactose-free milk': 'The first fully lactose-free milk, launched in September 2001 as a "light milk drink" because it could not be called milk; made by taking the lactose out with membranes rather than only by lactase (how exactly is disputed between sources)',
	'Minus L': 'Omira, Ireland and Germany; UHT, 1.5 % fat, 0.1 g lactose per 100 g',
	fairlife: 'Coca-Cola; launched in Minnesota in February 2014, nationally in February 2015; lactase is added, so it is lactose-free',
	Chigee: 'In Inner Mongolia the starter is a palm-sized cloth bag (hurunge) carrying the microbes of last year\'s chigee.',
	Trahanas: 'Greek trahanas has only cracked wheat or a couscous-like paste and fermented milk; Turkish tarhana adds yoghurt and vegetables, and Armenian tarkhana adds eggs.',
	Maconi: 'A kefir-like soured milk, made without grains (Table 2 of one review only)',
	Podkvasa: 'A kefir-like soured milk, made without grains (Table 2 of one review only)',
	'Thermised yoghurt': 'Heated to 60–70 °C for a few seconds, so it keeps 1–2 months chilled',
	'Ambient yoghurt': 'Heated to 75–110 °C for 5–20 s and filled aseptically, so it keeps 4–12 months at room temperature; sold as drinking yoghurt pouches',
	Ylette: 'The lighter version, 1.5 % fat',
	Gioddu: "Sardinian gioddu is listed in Italy's register of traditional food products; its starter, sa madrighe, is milk curdled with calf rennet",
	Zabady: 'Egyptian zabady: the milk is boiled hard first; the heat treatment is long (85 °C to boiling, 10 to 30 minutes), which thickens the milk before it is set',
	Meekiri: "Thicker, creamier and whiter than cow's-milk yoghurt; usually eaten with kithul (palm) or coconut treacle",
	Mabisi: 'Zambian mabisi is made in a calabash or clay pot, and in recent years often a plastic container, since calabashes are scarce; a field trial found no difference between the vessels',
	Nunu: "Made from raw cow's milk by spontaneous fermentation",
	Khuruud: 'Aarts (pressed curd) dried in the sun until rock-hard; a modern version presses unfermented curds in moulds; aaruul is the softer sweetened, sliced version of the same curd',
	Ititu: 'Built up in layers, not left in one batch.',
	'Fura da nono': 'Millet dough balls mashed into nono; Hausa, Fulani and Zarma-Songhai',
	Brukina: 'Ground millet and milk, Ghana (Northern Region); sources disagree on whether the milk is fermented: Wikipedia says pasteurised, a 2023 Discover Food paper lists it as a fermented milk-millet drink',
	'Dégué': 'Ground millet and milk, Senegal and Côte d\'Ivoire; sources disagree on whether the milk is fermented: Wikipedia says pasteurised for brukina, a 2023 Discover Food paper lists it as a fermented milk-millet drink',
	'Sweet cream butter': 'Süßrahmbutter: pH above 6.4, the cream is not soured',
	Kaymak: 'Balkan kajmak is salted and lightly soured',
	Tvorog: 'Made from whole milk',
	Kalari: 'Dogra and Gujjar cheese of Udhampur (Jammu): the curd is set with sour whey, not cooked like paneer, and dried in the sun; fried with salt and onion. Udhampur Kaladi has a GI tag (year given as 2021 or 2023 by different sources).',
	'Bandel cheese': 'Portuguese-era cheese from Bandel, Bengal: cow-milk curd set with lemon juice, moulded in small baskets, well salted; the brown kind is smoked',
	'Chhena poda': 'Chhena kneaded with sugar and semolina and baked, not cooked in syrup; the flavour is caramelised sugar',
	Pantua: 'The Bengali analogue of gulab jamun, made from chhena and semolina rather than khoa; Ledikeni is a lightly fried, syrup-filled kin',
